  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain returns Thursday as colder temperatures follow Friday\n into the weekend, with mountain snow and brisk conditions.\n\nDry and warm conditions continue this afternoon ahead of a\nstrong cold front impacting the forecast area on Thursday. A low\npressure system tracking from the Ohio River Valley and into the\nMid-Atlantic pushes the associated cold front across the area \non Thursday. Precipitation moves into the Alleghenies early \nThursday morning before tracking east through the day. QPF \nassociated with this system has overall trended downward with \nmuch of the area receiving a trace to a few tenths of an inch of\nprecip at most. Behind the front, brisk conditions are expected\nas winds gust around 20mph and high temperatures stay in the \n40s and 50s on Friday and Saturday. \n\nThe Alleghenies can expected to see their first accumulating\nsnowfall as northwest winds bring upslope snow showers to the\nregion. Light snowfall begins Thursday night as temperatures\nfall below freezing and continue through the day on Friday.\nMarginal temperatures likely limit accumulations during the day\non Friday to the highest ridges where temperatures remain below\nfreezing.Overall, the best chance for accumulating snow is \nFriday night which could result in travel disruptions as \nsurfaces get covered. Temperatures fall into the 20s and 30s \nFriday night. Total accumulations are expected to remain below \nWinter Weather Advisory criteria at this time. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cooler temperatures & dry condtions expected \nfor much of next week. \n\nAs the weekend's longwave trough slowly pushes offshore early next \nweek, winds could remain elevated through Sunday before diminishing \nbetween Monday & Tuesday in the wake of the cold front. Temperatures \nare likely to remain much cooler areawide throughout the week, with \nmany areas seeing near or below freezing temperatures overnight \nbeginning this weekend through the remainder of the forecast period.\n\nOverall, generally calm, clear conditions persist throughout the \nweek as a upper-level ridging transitions overhead. Daytime \ntemperatures moderate a bit later in the week with continued high \npressure, with afternoon highs in the mid-to-upper 50s on\nWednesday.",
